WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.813
Lecture three.

00:00:01.813 --> 00:00:03.113
Wind the coils.

00:00:04.313 --> 00:00:09.288
In the last lecture, we planned twelve coils and worked out the former setting.

00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:16.531
Today we wind them on the winding machine, one pull at a time, and lay each coil on the tray, ready for the stator.

00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.982
Here is the plan in the Winding Workshop.

00:00:19.982 --> 00:00:21.727
Twelve pulls, all the same.

00:00:21.727 --> 00:00:26.121
One hundred and thirty turns of zero point five six millimetre wire.

00:00:26.121 --> 00:00:31.872
The thirty-six millimetre step, with the pillars one hundred millimetres between centres.

00:00:32.673 --> 00:00:34.743
First, safety at the machine.

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:39.882
Hair tied back, no loose sleeves, and no gloves near the turning former.

00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:42.238
Eye protection when you cut wire.

00:00:42.238 --> 00:00:45.593
And stop the machine before you touch the wire.

00:00:45.593 --> 00:00:46.378
Every time.

00:00:47.173 --> 00:00:53.252
Before the first pull, weigh the reel and write down your prediction of the mass you will use.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:54.545
Then set the former.

00:00:54.545 --> 00:01:01.982
With the machine switched off, slide the pillars to one hundred millimetres between centres and lock the wing nuts.

00:01:01.982 --> 00:01:05.862
Wind one scrap turn on the step, take it off and measure it.

00:01:05.862 --> 00:01:09.289
You want about three hundred and fifteen millimetres.

00:01:10.093 --> 00:01:13.788
But the mean turn is three hundred and thirty millimetres.

00:01:13.788 --> 00:01:15.891
So why is the trial turn shorter?

00:01:15.891 --> 00:01:16.910
Pause and think.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.285
Because the trial turn sits on the bare step.

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:31.169
The mean turn sits halfway up the finished bundle, which is about five and a half millimetres high.

00:01:32.169 --> 00:01:33.933
Step two: thread the wire.

00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:36.987
Bring it through the tensioner and the guide.

00:01:36.987 --> 00:01:44.383
Leave a start lead of two hundred millimetres, slip on its sleeve, and clamp the lead under the anchor screw.

00:01:44.383 --> 00:01:47.640
Later, that lead becomes a joint, or a terminal.

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:49.690
Step three: wind.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:53.000
Preset the counter to one hundred and thirty.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.577
Wind the first five turns slowly, so the first layer lies flat; every layer above it follows the first.

00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:06.021
Then wind faster, with even tension, and slow down again for the last ten.

00:02:06.819 --> 00:02:11.095
Step four: tie the coil, while it is still on the former.

00:02:11.095 --> 00:02:16.196
Machine off, and keep the wire taut, so the last turn cannot unwind.

00:02:16.196 --> 00:02:18.897
Two ties, one on each straight side.

00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:22.781
What happens if you take a coil off without tying it?

00:02:22.781 --> 00:02:23.712
Pause and think.

00:02:29.412 --> 00:02:32.111
It springs open, and the turns cross.

00:02:32.111 --> 00:02:33.788
Then you wind it again.

00:02:34.788 --> 00:02:40.488
Step five: cut the wire two hundred millimetres past the coil, and slip on the end sleeve.

00:02:41.288 --> 00:02:47.793
Step six: unclamp the start lead from the anchor screw, release the pillars, and take the coil off.

00:02:47.793 --> 00:02:50.881
Hold it by the straight sides, not by the ends.

00:02:50.881 --> 00:02:55.678
Then slide the pillars back to one hundred millimetres for the next coil.

00:02:56.478 --> 00:03:01.600
Step seven: tag the coil and lay it on tray place one, start lead towards you.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:10.401
Scrape the enamel off the lead tips, and measure the resistance: about three ohms at twenty degrees, a little more in a warm workshop.

00:03:10.401 --> 00:03:16.114
The tray follows the insertion order, so the first coil you wind is the first to go in.

00:03:16.918 --> 00:03:21.698
Now the other eleven coils, in card order, all wound the same way round.

00:03:21.698 --> 00:03:25.283
Check the count before each coil comes off the former.

00:03:26.088 --> 00:03:32.042
Before you weigh the reel again, predict it: how much lighter will it be after twelve coils?

00:03:32.042 --> 00:03:33.077
Pause and think.

00:03:39.377 --> 00:03:41.129
Twelve coils on the tray.

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:42.601
Weigh the reel again.

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:51.011
The mass you lost is the wire in the winding: about one point one eight kilograms, about five hundred and twenty metres.

00:03:51.807 --> 00:03:55.553
So: thread, wind, tie, cut, release, and lay it out.

00:03:55.553 --> 00:03:58.363
Then the same again, eleven more times.

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:05.856
Before the next lecture, record the reel mass and the turn counts in worksheet four point three, part B.

